Looking at this, it appears it is designed to invert the usage of MediaTypeHeaderValue.

Today you would do something like this:
```cs
MetdiaTypeHeaderValue JsonMediaType = new MediaTypeHeaderValue("application/json");

// This allocates a new MediaTypeHeaderValue
if (MediaTypeHeaderValue.TryParse(request.ContentType, out var mt))
{
    return mt.IsSubsetOf(JsonMediaType);
}

return false;
```
Now you would do something like this:
```cs
MetdiaTypeHeaderValue JsonMediaType = new MediaTypeHeaderValue("application/json");

return JsonMediaType.MatchesMediaType(request.ContentType);
```
Is that correct?

To be honest I would probably continue to use [IsContentType](https://github.com/grpc/grpc-dotnet/blob/3ce9b104524a4929f5014c13cd99ba9a1c2431d4/src/Shared/CommonGrpcProtocolHelpers.cs#L26) for performance reasons. MatchesMediaType would functionally work, but it makes many more checks as it segments the content type string, and also checks for wildcards. I don't need that functionality.
